"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[88026],{888026:e=>{e.exports="# Power BI → UC Architecture Patterns\n\nReusable architecture patterns for Power BI → Unity Catalog metric view migrations. Use this skill when the right answer is an **architectural change**, not a one-off measure translation. The system prompt's Phase B (Decide architecture) routes you here before Phase C (Translate measures).\n\n## 1. Group measures by inferred DAX home, not by physical container\n\nReal Power BI semantic models commonly have one or more **measure container** tables — tables whose rows are dropped (Power BI's Enter-Data UI generates the empty `Table.FromRows(\"i44FAA==\", ...)` fingerprint) but which carry many DAX measures. The measures inside a single container often span multiple business domains (e.g. `_Measures` containing both Sales and Inventory metrics, or the `A_Measures_R&T` / `_P&E` / `_C&C` family with hundreds of measures each in a domain-grouped layout).\n\n**Do NOT reify a measure container as its own metric view.** Instead:\n\n1. For each measure inside the container, infer its **DAX home** — the Power BI **logical** fact table whose columns its expression actually aggregates over (`SUM(fact_csp[amount])` → home is `fact_csp`; `[A] - [B]` → home is the union of A's and B's homes; ultimately resolves to fact tables). \"Logical\" means the post-M-query, post-calculated-column table — the same row set DAX sees, not the raw upstream. The metric view's `source:` for that home is then resolved per \xa79 below: raw upstream only when the home table is PASSTHROUGH and has zero retained materializable calc columns (per `dax-patterns.md` \xa713.1) — pure-`RELATED` (\xa713.2) and untranslatable (\xa713.3) calc cols leave the host PASSTHROUGH; `v_<fact>` source view in every other case.\n2. Group measures by their inferred home into one metric view per fact-domain.\n3. Dimensions are shared across the resulting MVs via joins — see \xa78 below.\n4. A measure container that resolves to **no Databricks-resident fact data** (the model is self-contained `Table.FromRows`-only) cannot be migrated without user input. Trigger the 3-way upstream-resolution prompt from `source-views.md` so the user can supply explicit `catalog.schema.table` mappings or skip with reason `data_not_in_uc`.\n\nThis is the single most important architectural decision in a real Power BI migration. A na\xefve \"one MV per fact-table-with-measures\" rule produces 0 MVs for measure-container-only models, 1 over-stuffed MV for monolithic-container models, and over-fragmented MVs (one per container plus one per real fact) for models that mix the two patterns.\n\n## 2. Convert slicer-dispatch to separate measures or dashboard widgets\n\nWhen Power BI uses `SWITCH`, `SELECTEDVALUE`, or embedded selector tables to toggle:\n\n- value vs quantity\n- month vs prior month\n- actual vs plan\n- reporting period variants\n\nprefer:\n\n- **separate measures** for clearly named business variants (`sales_actual`, `sales_plan`); the dashboard slicer drives **which widget** to show, not which measure.\n- **filterable dimensions** when the toggle is truly a data attribute (e.g. `period_type ∈ {actual, plan}` is a fact-table column, not a dispatch).\n- **dashboard slicer + page-level filter** to reproduce the user-visible toggle. The metric view stays semantically clean; the user-facing presentation lives in the AI/BI dashboard.\n\nDo NOT attempt to preserve dynamic slicer dispatch as a metric-view expression. The metric view has no concept of \"visual context\" or \"selected slicer value\".\n\nFor the concrete DAX patterns you're decomposing, see `dax-patterns.md` \xa75–\xa76.\n\n## 3. Keep config scalars out of metric views when possible\n\nFor:\n\n- exchange rates\n- scaling factors (units / thousands / millions)\n- presentation-only toggles\n- selected version / period labels\n\nprefer:\n\n- **raw source units** in the metric view (e.g. `MEASURE(amount_local)` not `MEASURE(amount_local) / [exchange_rate]`).\n- **dashboard-layer formatting and presentation controls** (number formatters, slicer-bound parameters).\n- **explicit dimensions** when version selection is a legitimate slice (e.g. `version IN (CTM, CSP)` is a fact column, not a presentation toggle).\n\n## 4. Separate architecture problems from translation problems\n\nIf a measure is difficult because it crosses domains, ask first:\n\n- should the source tables be unified into a single source SQL view? (See \xa71 of `source-views.md`.)\n- should the measure be split into smaller measures?\n- should the logic become a dashboard-level control?\n- should the model expose a new dimension instead?\n\nIf the answer is yes, solve that here in Phase B and reduce Phase C complexity. The system prompt's \"this workflow is a guide, not a rigid sequence\" framing exists exactly for this — don't force translation when a structural change is the right answer.\n\n## 5. Cross-fact measures\n\nCross-fact measures (a single measure that combines aggregations from two or more fact tables) are legitimate `cannot_migrate` candidates when they require:\n\n- combining multiple fact tables in one measure expression (`SUM(fact_a[x]) - SUM(fact_b[y])`).\n- time-intelligence semantics across domains.\n- many-to-many or context-removal behavior that metric views cannot represent safely.\n\nBefore giving up, check whether a **shared staging view** would solve the problem structurally — combine the relevant fact tables into a single `v_<domain>_unified` source SQL view with a discriminator column, then build the metric view on top of that view. The composability frequently follows from there.\n\n## 6. Reference migration heuristics\n\nFrom successful Power BI migrations:\n\n- prefer a **small number of domain-shaped metric views** over many physical-shape MVs.\n- document every structural decision explicitly in the metric view's `comment:` field.\n- reduce view explosion by unifying structurally similar sources (see \xa71 of `source-views.md`).\n- let the metric view express **business facts**, not dashboard toggles.\n\n## 7. When to split instead of unify\n\nKeep separate metric views when:\n\n- the source schemas are materially different.\n- the business domain is genuinely different.\n- joins and dimensions diverge heavily.\n- a unified source would make downstream querying less understandable.\n\nThe goal is not the fewest possible views. The goal is the clearest domain architecture.\n\n## 8. Dimension completeness\n\nFor each metric view, **join ALL dimension tables** that the Power BI model relates to the fact table via active relationships. Do not cherry-pick a few dimensions.\n\nAudit process:\n\n1. Search `model.relationships[]` (via `regexFind` against the parsed JSON) for every relationship where the fact table is the `fromTable`.\n2. Each `toTable` that is a dimension should become a join in the metric view.\n3. Exclude: inactive relationships (`isActive: false`), self-joins, and relationships to other fact tables.\n4. Include: customer hierarchy (all levels), geography, channel, product attributes, calendar variants, store, and any other dimension the dashboard might filter or group by.\n\nThe goal: if a Power BI dashboard could filter by a dimension, the metric view should expose that dimension through a join.\n\nCommon miss: Power BI models with 15+ dimension tables per fact. All of them matter — downstream dashboards expect the same drill-down capabilities.\n\n## 9. Source-reference policy: where `source:` and `joins[].source:` point\n\nEvery metric view (and every join inside one) reads from one UC table or view. Which one depends on the underlying Power BI table's M-query bucket and on whether DAX calculated columns are attached. The principle is fixed: DAX measures, calculated columns, and relationships all execute against the **post-M-query semantic-model layer**, so the metric view must read the same row set.\n\n| Power BI table is…                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Phase B binds `source:` / `joins[].source:` to                                                                                                                                            | Lives under                                                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **PASSTHROUGH** — bare `Databricks.Catalogs(...){[Name=\"t\",Kind=\"Table\"]}` navigation **AND** zero retained materializable (\xa713.1) calc columns. Pure `RELATED(...)` lifts (\xa713.2 — become metric-view joins) and untranslatable shapes (\xa713.3 — cascade aggregating measures to `lod_fixed` while the column is dropped) both leave the host PASSTHROUGH; only \xa713.1 forces CREATE_VIEW. | the raw upstream's qualified name                                                                                                                                                         | `source_catalog.source_schema` (or the user-supplied per-table mapping from Phase B.1 option (b)) |\n| **CREATE_VIEW** — any non-trivial M-query (`Databricks.Catalogs(...) ▷ filter / select / derived columns`, `Value.NativeQuery`, real-data `Table.FromRows`, period-pair unification) **OR** at least one retained materializable calc column (`dax-patterns.md` \xa713.1: row-context-safe scalar shape) layered on top                                                                      | `<target_catalog>.<target_schema>.v_<table>` (the source view authored in Phase B.2)                                                                                                      | the target catalog/schema established at session start                                            |\n| **ASK** — non-Databricks upstream (`PowerPlatform.Dataflows(null)`, `entity` DirectQuery to AS, `Value.NativeQuery` against a non-Databricks data source, etc.)                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | resolved by the user's choice in Phase B.1: (a) and (b) yield concrete mappings that obey the same PASSTHROUGH-vs-CREATE_VIEW rule above; (c) skips the affected MV with `data_not_in_uc` | per (a)/(b) — usually `target_catalog.target_schema`                                              |\n\nCross-catalog setups (e.g. `db_database_pbi` for fact tables, `db_customer_master_data` for customer dimensions) follow the same rule per table — the source/target prefixes only choose the **namespace**, never the **layer**. Power BI parameter values themselves are runtime placeholders; substitute the concrete catalog/schema before any `createOrReplaceUCSourceView` / `createOrReplaceUCMetricView` call, and trigger the 3-way upstream-resolution prompt when you can't.\n\n**Joins inherit the rule per dimension.** A dimension table's join source follows whichever bucket _it_ falls into, independent of the fact's bucket:\n\n- A PASSTHROUGH fact joining a transformed (or \xa713.1-materializable-calc-column-carrying) dim references the dim's `v_<dim>` source view — never the raw dim. A pure-`RELATED`-only or \xa713.3-only dim stays PASSTHROUGH on its own; the join still references the raw dim directly.\n- A CREATE_VIEW fact joining a PASSTHROUGH dim references the raw dim directly.\n\nEach table is classified independently. The same rule applies to both `joins[].source:` (the join-target table) and the join's left-hand reference inside `joins[].on` (which always reads from the metric view's own `source:`, already chosen by the rule above).\n\n**Calc-column reclassification**, restated with the carve-out: a bare-PASSTHROUGH host moves to `CREATE_VIEW` _only_ when it carries at least one **materializable** calc column — a row-context-safe scalar per `dax-patterns.md` \xa713.1 (arithmetic, `IF` / `SWITCH` / `DIVIDE` over row fields, scalar text/date funcs, null coalescing). Pure `RELATED(...)` lifts (\xa713.2) leave the host PASSTHROUGH and become metric-view joins instead — the upstream already has every row column the metric view needs to read; the dim attribute surfaces through `joins[]`. Untranslatable calc-column DAX (\xa713.3 — aggregation funcs, `CALCULATE`, `FILTER`, measure refs, `EARLIER`, table-valued ops) also leaves the host PASSTHROUGH, but cascades every aggregating measure to `cannot_migrate` / `lod_fixed`. See `pbi-schema.md` \xa7\"Calculated columns\" rules 1 and 4.\n\n**Target locations** (recap). Source views (`v_<table>`) and metric views are written under the **target** catalog/schema (Phase 0). Source tables — including user-mapped per-table values from Phase B.1 — may live in different catalogs. Do not conflate. The actual DDL emission goes through `createOrReplaceUCSourceView` and `createOrReplaceUCMetricView`; never hand-build DDL strings, and never emit unresolved Power BI parameter placeholders (`{db_database_pbi}`, etc.).\n\n## 10. Config table policy\n\nFor `Table.FromRows` (EMBEDDED) tables that are **not** measure containers, decide based on dashboard need:\n\n**Generate a static source view** via `createOrReplaceUCSourceView` (with `selectStatement: 'SELECT * FROM VALUES (...) AS t(<col1>, <col2>, ...)'` — Spark SQL requires column aliases on inline VALUES; see `source-views.md` for the tool contract) when:\n\n- The table serves as a slicer backend (scaling factors, time-period selectors).\n- The table contains lookup data referenced by retained measures or dashboards.\n- The table provides configuration that dashboards will need at runtime.\n\n**Omit** when:\n\n- The table is purely internal Power BI display-layer formatting (custom sort orders, visual layout tables).\n- The table duplicates data already available as a dimension.\n\nFor the SQL shape of the static view see `source-views.md` \xa73.\n\n## 11. Report quality patterns\n\nThe migration report (your final assistant message) should include:\n\n1. **Inline YAML.** Embed the complete metric view YAML in the report in addition to the artifacts you wrote to UC. Stakeholders can review the YAML inline without opening the metric view editor.\n2. **Stakeholder action items.** End the report with a prioritized action table:\n\n   | #   | Action | Owner | Priority | Description |\n   | --- | ------ | ----- | -------- | ----------- |\n\n   Owner categories: Data Engineer, Platform Team, Business Analyst, Dashboard Developer, Analytics Engineer.\n   Priority: P0 (blocks deployment), P1 (pre-production), P2 (improvement).\n\n3. **YAML readability.** Use YAML block scalars (`|`) for multi-line `CASE` expressions in measure `expr:` fields. Avoids long single-line strings.\n4. **Example queries.** Include runnable examples using `MEASURE()` and `GROUP BY ALL` for the most important measures.\n"}}]);
